WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:10.060
In 1966, Joseph Weizenbaum at MIT unveiled a chat program called ELIZA.

2
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:17.970
Its trick was simple: find a keyword in what you typed, then follow written rules to turn your words back into a question.

3
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:29.520
Yet many people felt it understood them. Weizenbaum's own secretary reportedly asked him to leave the room so she could talk to ELIZA alone.

4
00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:35.670
Why would a program that only follows rules make people feel understood?

5
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:41.670
Today's AI talks far better. So what is AI, and what isn't it?

6
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:48.467
This is the first lesson of AI Literacy for Everyone: what AI is, and what it isn't.

7
00:00:48.817 --> 00:00:56.177
You'll learn to tell two things apart: talking, versus thinking and taking responsibility.

8
00:00:56.967 --> 00:01:03.807
First, what AI is. The OECD has a definition. In plain words:

9
00:01:04.157 --> 00:01:11.757
AI is a machine-based system that works out, from the input it receives, what output to give.

10
00:01:12.357 --> 00:01:18.157
Four common kinds of output: predictions, recommendations, content and decisions.

11
00:01:18.507 --> 00:01:30.187
Your map app guessing when you'll arrive is a prediction; the next short video is a recommendation; a chatbot's paragraph is content; face unlock is a decision.

12
00:01:30.537 --> 00:01:39.977
ELIZA's skills were all in rules people wrote. Most of today's AI is different: it learns its skills from large amounts of data.

13
00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:45.887
Now, what AI isn't. Remember three 'not the same as'.

14
00:01:46.237 --> 00:01:54.837
One: talking is not understanding. 'I understand how you feel' doesn't mean it understands or cares.

15
00:01:55.187 --> 00:02:04.947
Two: answering is not knowing. Google's chatbot got the first exoplanet photo wrong in its launch demo.

16
00:02:05.297 --> 00:02:17.657
Three: acting is not being responsible. An airline in Canada argued its website chatbot was responsible for what it said. The tribunal disagreed and ordered the airline to pay.

17
00:02:18.007 --> 00:02:24.807
What a machine does, people answer for: the people who use it, and the organisation that put it to work.

18
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:30.360
Let's play an unplugged game: guess the rule. This is a demo.

19
00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:38.190
One person is the machine, holding a rule card, for example: if the word is an animal, raise your left hand.

20
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:48.300
Everyone else calls out words. Kitten: left hand. Table: nothing. Tiger: left hand.

21
00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:59.370
Got the rule? The machine is fast and accurate, but it doesn't know kittens are cute or tigers are dangerous. It just follows the rule.

22
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:08.560
Today's AI learns its rules from data instead of being given them, but the point is the same: getting it right is not understanding.

23
00:03:09.334 --> 00:03:19.814
Three things to remember: AI turns inputs into outputs; talking isn't understanding and answering isn't knowing; people answer for what it does.

24
00:03:20.164 --> 00:03:30.164
Quick exercise: count how many times you use AI today. Each time, is it predicting, recommending, generating content, or deciding?

25
00:03:30.514 --> 00:03:36.954
Next time, C2: where AI's answers come from, and why it sometimes makes things up.
